		},
		{
			"id": "tofGwmjWw09Z_dSFT",
			"name": "Leaping Musculature",
			"reference": "B89",
			"local_notes": "Doubles jumping distance (Basic's Super Jump rule, B89/P79): a Hormagaunt can clear a full-grown man in a standing jump and closes the last few yards to a target in a single leaping bound rather than a run.\n\nLore: Hormagaunts are built specifically to close distance faster than almost anything else in the swarm, covering ground in long, bounding leaps rather than a flat sprint — a consistent visual in Codex background and Black Library's Hive Fleet material alike.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Super Jump (B89/P79, 10 points/level), level 1, 10 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"points_per_level": 10,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "txp85wLCT5XC7iROX",
			"name": "Enhanced Move (Ground)",
			"reference": "B52",
			"local_notes": "Enhanced Move (Ground) 1/2: top sprinting speed x1.5. Combat Move unchanged.\n\nLore: Hormagaunts are bred to cover ground faster than almost anything else in the swarm, a flat-out bounding charge that reaches the line before defenders can reposition -- Codex background's consistent framing of the bioform.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Enhanced Move (Ground) 1/2 (B52, half level 10 points), the same half level this project's Astartes and Aeldari files use. With Basic Move 12 (bought in the Attribute Modifiers) a Hormagaunt sprints at Move 18, matching a Space Marine's top speed.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"points_per_level": 20,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 0.5
		},
		{
			"id": "tP5WTv_49oKXflpBH",
			"name": "Clinging",
			"reference": "B43",
			"local_notes": "Walks or crawls up walls and across ceilings at half Move, no Climbing roll.\n\nLore: Genestealers and Lictors move through a ruin as easily up its walls as along its floor: Space Hulk's broods come out of the ceiling vents, and the Lictor waits on the underside of a gantry or the face of a hab-block until its prey walks beneath. Codex background has both scale sheer surfaces with hooked claws and barbed limbs.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Clinging (B43, 20 points), unmodified.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"base_points": 20
		},
		{
			"id": "txp85wLCT5XC7iROX",
			"name": "Enhanced Move (Ground)",
			"reference": "B52",
			"local_notes": "Enhanced Move (Ground) 1: doubles ground Move for sprinting (Move 14 becomes 28). Combat Move unchanged.\n\nLore: A Genestealer is consistently described and depicted as faster than almost anything it hunts — closing on a target from cover in a fraction of a second, per Codex background's \"fastest killer in the swarm\" framing and every Black Library depiction of a Genestealer infiltration going wrong for its prey.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Enhanced Move (Ground) 1 (B52, 20 points), the same trait and rate this project's Astartes, Custodes and Aeldari files use for their own speed anchors, at a full level rather than the half-level those files use. Together with Basic Move 14 (bought in the Attribute Modifiers) a Genestealer walks at Move 14 and sprints at Move 28; a Space Marine has Move 12 with Enhanced Move 1/2 and sprints at 18, so a Genestealer outruns even a Space Marine on foot, in both Basic Move and top speed.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"points_per_level": 20,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "tyKxR2NwfvJSADYmw",
			"name": "Infravision",
			"reference": "B60",
			"local_notes": "Sees body heat; ignores darkness and most concealment based on visible light alone.\n\nLore: Genestealers hunt as often in ductwork, hab-block crawlspaces and unlit holds as in daylight, and Codex background is explicit that darkness is no protection from one.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Infravision (B60/P87, 10 points), unmodified.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"base_points": 10
		},
		{
			"id": "tpMplJvLr6PFKzwgd",
			"name": "Extra Attack (Second Claw)",
			"reference": "B53",
			"local_notes": "One extra Attack maneuver per turn, using its own rending claws (see the Rending Claws weapon-symbiote for the actual attack line).\n\nLore: A Genestealer's four clawed limbs strike in a blur that has no real human equivalent — every Codex-era description and the classic \"it's already inside the room\" Black Library beat treats a Genestealer as attacking far more than once per second of combat.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Extra Attack (B53/P49, 25 points/level), level 1, 25 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"points_per_level": 25,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "tQi47Zh-umTKlA-tf",
			"name": "Extra Attack (Scything Talons)",
			"reference": "B53",
			"local_notes": "One extra attack per turn: the Lictor's two great scything talons strike as its rending claws (see the Rending Claws weapon-symbiote for the attack line).\n\nLore: A Lictor kills with a flurry from its long scything talons and clawed arms, taking a sentry apart in a second; the Codex and Black Library treat a lone Lictor among a squad of Guardsmen as a slaughter, not a duel.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Extra Attack (B53/P49, 25 points/level), level 1, 25 points (user direction).",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"points_per_level": 25,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "tezIdI5c1nQbxOwPp",
			"name": "Tunnelling",
			"reference": "B94",
			"local_notes": "Tunnel Move 7 through loose earth, sand, or similar soft ground only (never solid rock or a hardened floor), submerging and re-surfacing as a single Move action; it may not attack while submerged. The moment it erupts from the ground it is treated as attacking from ambush for that turn's initial attack.\n\nLore: Raveners burrow beneath the battlefield and erupt without warning beneath their target, the signature ambush Codex background gives the bioform and a recurring beat in Black Library's Hive Fleet material and Deathwatch's own Mark of the Xenos description of the species.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Tunneling (B94, 30 base + 5/level of Tunnel Move) at Tunnel Move 7 = 65, with an Accessibility limitation restricting it to soft earth only (-40%, an eyeballed percentage in line with this project's own Accessibility convention): 65 x 0.6 = 39 points. This replaces an earlier Enhanced Move build (12 points), which doubled an existing Move rather than granting a burrowing mode at all. Tunnel Move is a separate figure from Basic Move (12 for a Ravener): it is set to 7 as a design choice, burrowing through soil being slower than running, and does not track Basic Move.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "mxrfA9DQUpfOcGzvT",
					"name": "Accessibility (soft earth, sand and similar loose ground only)",
					"cost_adj": "-40%"
				}
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 7,
			"base_points": 30
		},
		{
			"id": "tQ1uSq0pz7JdTHs7K",
			"name": "Chameleonic Hide",
			"reference": "B41",
			"local_notes": "-4 to others' Vision rolls to spot the Lictor while it is motionless, or -2 while moving (per Basic's Chameleon rule, B41); does not work against Infravision, scent-based tracking, or other non-visual detection.\n\nLore: A Lictor's hide shifts colour and texture to match its surroundings well enough to stand motionless in plain sight and go unnoticed until it strikes — the bioform's whole reason for existing in Codex background, and the source of its reputation as the swarm's perfect assassin in Black Library's Hive Fleet fiction.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Chameleon (B41/P43, 5 points/level), level 4, 20 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"points_per_level": 5,
			"can_level": true,
			"levels": 4
		},
		{
			"id": "t-q_oOw79L8hLXVXr",
			"name": "Pheromone Trail",
			"local_notes": "Automatically marks the Lictor's path and kill sites with a chemical trail other Tyranid organisms can follow without a roll, for as long as roughly a day; the GM may use this to have the nearest swarm elements arrive at a Lictor's last known location once its scouting or killing is done.\n\nLore: Lictors scout ahead of a landing and leave a chemical trail for the rest of the Hive Fleet to follow to a viable kill zone — standard Codex background on the bioform's actual battlefield purpose (infiltration and target-marking, not sustained combat).\n\nDesign: Perk (B21, 1 point): a narrow, always-on tracking convenience for other Tyranids rather than a combat effect, priced the same way this project's other Perk-tier flavour traits (Necron's \"Beneath a Daemon's Notice,\" this file's own Klan-style flags) are.",
			"tags": [
				"Perk",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"base_points": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "tyiVDFWvqZdKquDyL",
			"name": "Regeneration (Adapted Biomass)",
			"reference": "B80",
			"local_notes": "Regeneration (Regular): recovers 1 HP per hour even without rest or first aid.\n\nLore: Larger Tyranid bioforms visibly close wounds and regrow damaged tissue over the course of a battle, part of the same adaptive biology that lets the Hive Fleet field bioforms built to take punishment no unaugmented creature could survive. Codex background across editions gives this trait explicitly to Tyranid monstrous creatures.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Regeneration (Regular) (B80, 25 points).",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"base_points": 25
		},
		{
			"id": "tKA_GOrM66BTlmHcc",
			"name": "Regeneration (Adapted Biomass, Fast)",
			"reference": "B80",
			"local_notes": "Regeneration (Fast): recovers 1 HP per minute even without rest or first aid.\n\nLore: A Carnifex is built to absorb an amount of damage that would kill any other bioform several times over and keep coming, closing wounds visibly fast enough to matter mid-fight — Codex background's consistent framing of the Carnifex as the swarm's most durable single-target answer to armour.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Regeneration (Fast) (B80, 50 points), one tier above the Hive Tyrant's Regular rate, matching the Carnifex's specific reputation for shrugging off damage that would down a Hive Tyrant.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"base_points": 50
		},
		{
			"id": "t3OUKKHUj7GENLnYr",
			"name": "Warp Field (Bio-Psychic Ward)",
			"local_notes": "A regenerating psychic buffer, built on the same Shield Points mechanic framework.md's \"Personal force fields\" section uses for worn refractor fields and the like: SP 200, 2-second delay, recharges 25 SP/second once undamaged for that long, covers all locations. Damage comes off this pool first (armour divisors do not apply to SP); once it is exhausted, excess damage carries on to the Zoanthrope's chitin at the attack's full armour divisor, and the field needs a full 4 seconds undamaged before it starts recharging from empty. Track current SP with a Uses counter, as for worn fields.\n\nLore: A Zoanthrope generates a visible, crackling field of raw psychic force around its frail body — its only real protection beyond its own thin chitin, and Codex background's explanation for why a Zoanthrope can survive fire that would kill it outright without one. On the tabletop it has long been one of the best invulnerable saves in the game (3+ for most editions), better than a Terminator's storm shield, so bolter and lasgun fire spatters off it and only massed or heavy weapons break through.\n\nDesign: This project has no published point-cost formula for a shield's SP pool (framework.md tracks worn fields as equipment with a Uses counter, not as a priced character trait); a Zoanthrope's field is part of its body rather than worn gear, so it needs an honest point price here. Eyeballed at half of Damage Resistance's own rate (B46, 5 points per point of permanent DR) for a pool that absorbs the same amount of damage but is temporary and must recharge, i.e. 0.5 points per SP: 200 SP x 0.5 = 100 points. Raised from 60 SP (30 points, the Rosarius anchor) at the user's direction, because 60 SP broke to two bolts and the lore field outlasts a storm shield: 200 SP sits above framework.md's storm shield (160 SP, 20/s) and recharges at 25 SP/second, so a squad's bolters must keep firing to wear it down, while a lascannon (8dx8(5), about 224) still breaks it in one shot. The 4-second restart after collapse is kept. This is a reasoned extrapolation from Basic Set principles, not a catalogue trait renamed, and a future framework update that adds a formal shield- point pricing formula should supersede it.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"base_points": 100
		},
		{
			"id": "tUNMDsXFB0G8xKGgk",
			"name": "Wings (Flight)",
			"reference": "B56",
			"local_notes": "Disabled by default; enable for a winged Hive Tyrant variant. Air Move equals twice Basic Speed (dropping fractions), per Basic's Flight rule; Winged flight cannot hover and is grounded by high winds or a damaged wing, same as any other winged flier.\n\nLore: Some Hive Tyrants are grown with a huge, membranous wing pair for genuine flight rather than the leaping glides other large bioforms manage — a well-known wargear/bioform option across Codex editions and a striking image in Black Library's Hive Fleet material whenever a Winged Hive Tyrant appears.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Flight (B56, 40 points) with the Winged limitation (-25%, B56, cannot hover, grounded by bad weather or wing damage): 40 x 0.75 = 30 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"modifiers": [
				{
					"id": "miX7BfPrpS9rRhPBn",
					"name": "Winged",
					"reference": "B56",
					"cost_adj": "-25%"
				}
			],
			"base_points": 40,
			"disabled": true
		},
		{
			"id": "TWu_fWbHNjDgI9A7R",
			"name": "Body Lens: Termagant",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Termagant: the Hive Fleet's basic gun-beast, grown cheaply and in huge numbers to advance in a firing line ahead of, or alongside, heavier bioforms. Consistent across Codex background and Black Library's Hive Fleet fiction as the swarm's most disposable ranged infantry.\n\nDesign: -90 (Attribute Modifiers) + 125 (Chitin DR 25, 5/level) = 35 points, added to Tyranid Biology's 20 for 55 points of physiology alone before skills and its weapon-symbiote. See the Termagant template. NPC creature stat block only, not a player-facing race; the point total below is for GM reference, not party balance.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t44PYGG0-xeeQlixe",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Termagant)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target: SM -1, ST 9, DX 11, IQ 4, HT 11, HP 9. Decreased ST -1 (B14, 10/level, -10) for ST 9. Increased DX +1 (B15, 20/level, 20) for DX 11 -- a gun-beast bred for a steady aim despite its small frame. Decreased IQ -6 (B16, 20/level, -120) for IQ 4, barely above pure animal cunning, matching a creature with essentially no independent judgement outside the Hive Mind Link. Increased HT +1 (B15, 10/level, 10) for HT 11. Base HP from ST 9 is already 9, the framework target, so no separate Extra Hit Points purchase. SM -1 carries no independent point cost, per this project's Ork Gretchin precedent (no ST premium is applied for a negative Size Modifier). Increased Basic Move +2 (B17, 5/level, 10): Basic Speed 5.5 gives Move 5, so +2 lands the swarm's skirmishing gun-beast at Move 7, a brisk trot that keeps a firing line advancing. -10+20-120+10+10 = -90.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": -90,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "sm",
							"amount": -1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": -1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "dx",
							"amount": 1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": -6
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "ht",
							"amount": 1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "basic_move",
							"amount": 2
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tWMU_UJPksR_Apujc",
					"name": "Chitin (Termagant)",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 25, all locations. Weak Points 7 (thin, gappy plates on a swarm-scale gun-beast, per framework.md's own \"flak-tier\" Weak Points figure for the loosest armour in this project): on a hit that fails to penetrate, roll 3d; 7 or less halves DR (13, rounded down) against that hit. Eye clusters run at DR 12 (roughly half the body DR) rather than 25.\n\nLore: A Tyranid's exoskeleton is grown, not worn: dense bio-ceramic chitin fused directly to the flesh beneath it, thickest on the largest bioforms and thin enough on a Termagant to be more like a swarm-trooper's flak jacket than real armour plate. Universal across Codex background and every piece of Black Library Tyranid fiction.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance (B46, 5/level, all locations): natural DR bought directly on the body, per framework.md's explicit instruction that chitin is a character trait, not equipment. Each body-type lens overrides `levels`/`dr` to its own figure from framework.md's Tyranid anchor table; see that lens's own copy of this trait for its point cost.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 25,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"all"
							],
							"amount": 25
						}
					]
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TwgAkJviNqvEaM12s",
			"name": "Body Lens: Hormagaunt",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Hormagaunt: a close-combat swarm killer bred for speed over firepower, closing on its target in long bounding leaps rather than a flat sprint before its scything talons go to work. Codex background across editions and Black Library's Hive Fleet fiction both treat Hormagaunts as the swarm's fastest melee threat at this tier.\n\nDesign: 0 (Attribute Modifiers) + 125 (Chitin DR 25) + 10 (Leaping Musculature) + 10 (Enhanced Move 1/2) = 145 points, added to Tyranid Biology's 20 for 145 points of physiology alone before skills and its weapon-symbiote. See the Hormagaunt template. NPC creature stat block only.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tY6LXTx6VgasyMPQz",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Hormagaunt)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target: SM -1, ST 12, DX 13, IQ 4, HT 11, HP 12. Increased ST +2 (B14, 10/level, 20) for ST 12 (strength ladder: a Hormagaunt is a lean, wiry killer, a little stronger than a human; no discount, per the negative-SM note below). Increased DX +3 (B15, 20/level, 60) for DX 13 -- a bounding, close-combat killer well past a Termagant's steadier aim. Decreased IQ -6 (-120) for IQ 4, as debased as a Termagant's. Increased HT +1 (10) for HT 11. Base HP from ST 12 is already 12, the framework target, so no separate Extra Hit Points purchase. SM -1 carries no independent cost, as the Termagant lens above. Increased Basic Move +6 (B17, 5/level, 30): Basic Speed 6.0 gives Move 6, so +6 makes Basic Move 12, the swarm's fastest sprinter short of a Genestealer (see also Enhanced Move 1/2 below). 20+60-120+10+30 = 0.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 0,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "sm",
							"amount": -1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": 2
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "dx",
							"amount": 3
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": -6
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "ht",
							"amount": 1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "basic_move",
							"amount": 6
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tmTNuhwyrs5uRVBPV",
					"name": "Chitin (Hormagaunt)",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 25, all locations. Weak Points 7 (as thin and gappy as a Termagant's plating, per framework.md's flak-tier figure): on a hit that fails to penetrate, roll 3d; 7 or less halves DR (13, rounded down). Eye clusters run at DR 12 rather than 25.\n\nLore: A Tyranid's exoskeleton is grown, not worn: dense bio-ceramic chitin fused directly to the flesh beneath it, thickest on the largest bioforms and thin enough on a Termagant to be more like a swarm-trooper's flak jacket than real armour plate. Universal across Codex background and every piece of Black Library Tyranid fiction.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance (B46, 5/level, all locations): natural DR bought directly on the body, per framework.md's explicit instruction that chitin is a character trait, not equipment. Each body-type lens overrides `levels`/`dr` to its own figure from framework.md's Tyranid anchor table; see that lens's own copy of this trait for its point cost.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 25,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"all"
							],
							"amount": 25
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tf3Qi3HRxJuH7razp",
					"name": "Leaping Musculature",
					"reference": "B89",
					"local_notes": "Doubles jumping distance (Basic's Super Jump rule, B89/P79): a Hormagaunt can clear a full-grown man in a standing jump and closes the last few yards to a target in a single leaping bound rather than a run.\n\nLore: Hormagaunts are built specifically to close distance faster than almost anything else in the swarm, covering ground in long, bounding leaps rather than a flat sprint — a consistent visual in Codex background and Black Library's Hive Fleet material alike.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Super Jump (B89/P79, 10 points/level), level 1, 10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 10,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1
				},
				{
					"id": "tnB6PSBQyNHj9yfLC",
					"name": "Enhanced Move (Ground)",
					"reference": "B52",
					"local_notes": "Enhanced Move (Ground) 1/2: top sprinting speed x1.5. Combat Move unchanged.\n\nLore: Hormagaunts are bred to cover ground faster than almost anything else in the swarm, a flat-out bounding charge that reaches the line before defenders can reposition -- Codex background's consistent framing of the bioform.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Enhanced Move (Ground) 1/2 (B52, half level 10 points), the same half level this project's Astartes and Aeldari files use. With Basic Move 12 (bought in the Attribute Modifiers) a Hormagaunt sprints at Move 18, matching a Space Marine's top speed.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 20,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 0.5
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "Td15AbbMSEXPDWg8B",
			"name": "Body Lens: Genestealer",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Genestealer: the fastest, most dangerous close-quarters killer the swarm fields at this tier, and the origin point of a broodline infestation that can subvert a whole world before the main Hive Fleet arrives. Consistent Codex background across editions and a recurring Black Library horror-fiction staple (an infiltrating Genestealer moving faster than anyone in the room can react).\n\nDesign: 301 (Attribute Modifiers) + 250 (Chitin DR 50) + 20 (Enhanced Move 1) + 20 (Clinging) + 10 (Infravision) + 25 (Extra Attack) + 45 (Lightning Reflexes) + 50 (Injury Tolerance 2) = 721 points, added to Tyranid Biology's 20 for 741 points of physiology alone before skills and its weapon-symbiote. See the Genestealer template. NPC creature stat block only; a Genestealer's broodline-infestation role is a GM plot hook, not a point-costed trait.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tTFICpwt3t5Ju5jsV",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Genestealer)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target: SM 0, ST 26, DX 17, IQ 6, HT 13, HP 34. Increased ST +16 (B14, 10/level, 160) for ST 26 (strength ladder: a Necron Warrior's strength, short of a Marine's 30). Increased DX +7 (B15, 20/level, 140) for DX 17 -- past an Astartes Battle- Brother's own precision, the fiction's \"fastest killer in the swarm\" made literal. Decreased IQ -4 (B16, 20/level, -80) for IQ 6 -- animal-cunning-plus, enough for a genuinely dangerous infiltration predator without any real reasoning. Increased HT +3 (B15, 10/level, 30) for HT 13. Extra Hit Points +8 (B16, 2/level, 16) on top of the ST-26 base of 26, for HP 34, a Marine's: the tabletop gives both two wounds. Increased Basic Move +7 (B17, 5/level, 35): Basic Speed is (17+13)/4 = 7.5, Move 7, so +7 lands Basic Move at exactly 14. 160+140-80+30+16+35 = 301.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 301,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": 16
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "dx",
							"amount": 7
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": -4
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "ht",
							"amount": 3
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "hp",
							"amount": 8
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "basic_move",
							"amount": 7
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tO_vtw4PN8yP9xHNh",
					"name": "Chitin (Genestealer)",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 50, all locations. Weak Points 5 (tighter, more developed plating than a swarm gaunt's, on a par with Guard-pattern carapace, per framework.md's own figure for that tier): on a hit that fails to penetrate, roll 3d; 5 or less halves DR (25). Eye clusters run at DR 25 rather than 50.\n\nLore: A Tyranid's exoskeleton is grown, not worn: dense bio-ceramic chitin fused directly to the flesh beneath it, thickest on the largest bioforms and thin enough on a Termagant to be more like a swarm-trooper's flak jacket than real armour plate. Universal across Codex background and every piece of Black Library Tyranid fiction.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance (B46, 5/level, all locations): natural DR bought directly on the body, per framework.md's explicit instruction that chitin is a character trait, not equipment. Each body-type lens overrides `levels`/`dr` to its own figure from framework.md's Tyranid anchor table; see that lens's own copy of this trait for its point cost.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 50,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"all"
							],
							"amount": 50
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tXo7B3uBhHlJ94uwL",
					"name": "Enhanced Move (Ground)",
					"reference": "B52",
					"local_notes": "Enhanced Move (Ground) 1: doubles ground Move for sprinting (Move 14 becomes 28). Combat Move unchanged.\n\nLore: A Genestealer is consistently described and depicted as faster than almost anything it hunts — closing on a target from cover in a fraction of a second, per Codex background's \"fastest killer in the swarm\" framing and every Black Library depiction of a Genestealer infiltration going wrong for its prey.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Enhanced Move (Ground) 1 (B52, 20 points), the same trait and rate this project's Astartes, Custodes and Aeldari files use for their own speed anchors, at a full level rather than the half-level those files use. Together with Basic Move 14 (bought in the Attribute Modifiers) a Genestealer walks at Move 14 and sprints at Move 28; a Space Marine has Move 12 with Enhanced Move 1/2 and sprints at 18, so a Genestealer outruns even a Space Marine on foot, in both Basic Move and top speed.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 20,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1
				},
				{
					"id": "t3bBuo7KyILKi7LcF",
					"name": "Clinging",
					"reference": "B43",
					"local_notes": "Walks or crawls up walls and across ceilings at half Move, no Climbing roll.\n\nLore: Genestealers and Lictors move through a ruin as easily up its walls as along its floor: Space Hulk's broods come out of the ceiling vents, and the Lictor waits on the underside of a gantry or the face of a hab-block until its prey walks beneath. Codex background has both scale sheer surfaces with hooked claws and barbed limbs.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Clinging (B43, 20 points), unmodified.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"base_points": 20
				},
				{
					"id": "tznytQ8s5VcMvkSw0",
					"name": "Infravision",
					"reference": "B60",
					"local_notes": "Sees body heat; ignores darkness and most concealment based on visible light alone.\n\nLore: Genestealers hunt as often in ductwork, hab-block crawlspaces and unlit holds as in daylight, and Codex background is explicit that darkness is no protection from one.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Infravision (B60/P87, 10 points), unmodified.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tVCOFGOYD5tzILX6G",
					"name": "Extra Attack (Second Claw)",
					"reference": "B53",
					"local_notes": "One extra Attack maneuver per turn, using its own rending claws (see the Rending Claws weapon-symbiote for the actual attack line).\n\nLore: A Genestealer's four clawed limbs strike in a blur that has no real human equivalent — every Codex-era description and the classic \"it's already inside the room\" Black Library beat treats a Genestealer as attacking far more than once per second of combat.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Extra Attack (B53/P49, 25 points/level), level 1, 25 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 25,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1
				},
				{
					"id": "TxLAIbURc1bAnwCot",
					"name": "Lightning Reflexes",
					"local_notes": "Lore: Codex: Tyranids gives Genestealers \"Lightning Reflexes\": they are not where the shot went. Space Hulk's Genestealers cross a corridor faster than a Terminator can track them.\n\nDesign: Combat Reflexes (B43) 15 and Enhanced Dodge 2 (B51, 15/level) 30 = 45 points. Dodge 10 becomes 13.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"container_type": "meta_trait",
					"children": [
						{
							"id": "tjXw1uNvn97srhBag",
							"name": "Combat Reflexes",
							"reference": "B43",
							"tags": [
								"Advantage",
								"Mental"
							],
							"base_points": 15
						},
						{
							"id": "tCen2IBTyjvDOsNZM",
							"name": "Enhanced Dodge",
							"reference": "B51",
							"tags": [
								"Advantage",
								"Mental"
							],
							"points_per_level": 15,
							"can_level": true,
							"levels": 2,
							"base_points": 0
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "t_FNLjZWD2ggq3dME",
					"name": "Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction)",
					"reference": "P53",
					"local_notes": "Divisor 2 (level = divisor): after DR and wounding multipliers, all injury is divided by 2 (round down, minimum 1 if anything penetrated).\n\nLore: Redundant organs in a body built to keep killing: on the tabletop a Genestealer takes a bolter round as a Marine does, one of two wounds.\n\nDesign: Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction) (GURPS Powers p. 53, 25 points per level, level = divisor): divisor 2 = 50 points. Without it a bolt to the vitals averaged two to four times a Genestealer's HP.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 25,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 2,
					"base_points": 0
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TMRK2SY18mSkWMviA",
			"name": "Body Lens: Ravener",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Ravener: a serpentine burrowing predator that erupts from beneath a defensive line rather than crossing open ground to reach it. Codex background across editions is explicit that this ambush capability, not raw combat stats, is the Ravener's whole purpose.\n\nDesign: 251 (Attribute Modifiers) + 300 (Chitin DR 60) + 39 (Tunnelling) + 20 (Shadow in the Warp) + 50 (Injury Tolerance 2) = 660 points, added to Tyranid Biology's 20 for 680 points of physiology alone before skills and its weapon- symbiote. See the Ravener template. NPC creature stat block only.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t2QuYQMLNAK0yn3HV",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Ravener)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target: SM +1, ST 32, DX 14, IQ 5, HT 14, HP 36. Increased ST +22 (B14, 10/level, 220) for ST 32 (strength ladder: a Primaris Marine's strength), with the SM +1 cost break (-10% per B18's Size and Strength Table for a creature whose bulk matches its size, the same discount this project's Ork Warboss and Custodes Attribute Modifiers entries take): 220 x 0.9 = 198. Increased DX +4 (B15, 20/level, 80) for DX 14 -- a serpentine burrowing predator, quick despite its bulk. Decreased IQ -5 (B16, 20/level, -100) for IQ 5, barely above a Termagant's. Increased HT +4 (B15, 10/level, 40) for HT 14. Extra Hit Points +4 (B16, 2/level, 8) on top of the ST-32 base of 32, for HP 36. Increased Basic Move +5 (B17, 5/level, 25): Basic Speed 7.0 gives Move 7, so +5 lands Basic Move at 12. 198+80-100+40+8+25 = 251.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 251,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "sm",
							"amount": 1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": 22
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "dx",
							"amount": 4
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": -5
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "ht",
							"amount": 4
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "hp",
							"amount": 4
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "basic_move",
							"amount": 5
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "teBNB16kXWKYuiegM",
					"name": "Chitin (Ravener)",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 60, all locations. Weak Points 5 (as a Genestealer's, matching framework.md's own figure for this tier): on a hit that fails to penetrate, roll 3d; 5 or less halves DR (30). Eye clusters run at DR 30 rather than 60.\n\nLore: A Tyranid's exoskeleton is grown, not worn: dense bio-ceramic chitin fused directly to the flesh beneath it, thickest on the largest bioforms and thin enough on a Termagant to be more like a swarm-trooper's flak jacket than real armour plate. Universal across Codex background and every piece of Black Library Tyranid fiction.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance (B46, 5/level, all locations): natural DR bought directly on the body, per framework.md's explicit instruction that chitin is a character trait, not equipment. Each body-type lens overrides `levels`/`dr` to its own figure from framework.md's Tyranid anchor table; see that lens's own copy of this trait for its point cost.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 60,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"all"
							],
							"amount": 60
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tIFYjf1vxfUs3mBcS",
					"name": "Tunnelling",
					"reference": "B94",
					"local_notes": "Tunnel Move 7 through loose earth, sand, or similar soft ground only (never solid rock or a hardened floor), submerging and re-surfacing as a single Move action; it may not attack while submerged. The moment it erupts from the ground it is treated as attacking from ambush for that turn's initial attack.\n\nLore: Raveners burrow beneath the battlefield and erupt without warning beneath their target, the signature ambush Codex background gives the bioform and a recurring beat in Black Library's Hive Fleet material and Deathwatch's own Mark of the Xenos description of the species.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Tunneling (B94, 30 base + 5/level of Tunnel Move) at Tunnel Move 7 = 65, with an Accessibility limitation restricting it to soft earth only (-40%, an eyeballed percentage in line with this project's own Accessibility convention): 65 x 0.6 = 39 points. This replaces an earlier Enhanced Move build (12 points), which doubled an existing Move rather than granting a burrowing mode at all. Tunnel Move is a separate figure from Basic Move (12 for a Ravener): it is set to 7 as a design choice, burrowing through soil being slower than running, and does not track Basic Move.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mhJ9u1rc-7PgEzrMu",
							"name": "Accessibility (soft earth, sand and similar loose ground only)",
							"cost_adj": "-40%"
						}
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 7,
					"base_points": 30
				},
				{
					"id": "tS4CiAGeG14krm2iu",
					"name": "Shadow in the Warp (Ravener)",
					"reference": "B35",
					"local_notes": "Any psyker (or other warp-sensitive mind) within 8 yards suffers -3 to Psyniscience, Warp Sorcery-tagged skill rolls, and the Will roll to resist Perils of the Warp, and must make an immediate Fright Check the first time they come within range of this creature. Non-psychic minds are unaffected.\n\nLore: The Hive Mind's raw psychic mass smothers the immaterium around any Tyranid bioform large enough to carry a fraction of it — Codex background describes Imperial and Aeldari psykers alike going deaf, blind, or outright panicking the moment a large Tyranid force (never just the smallest gaunts) comes close enough to sense. Deathwatch's Mark of the Xenos material treats Shadow in the Warp as a standing hazard for any Librarian escorting a kill-team against Tyranids.\n\nDesign: Affliction (B35) base 10; Disadvantage Delivered (a compound -30 point disadvantage — the skill/Empowerment penalty plus the forced Fright Check — valued per this project's own eyeballed Disadvantage Delivered convention, +30%); Area Effect at the 8-yard tier (+150%, B102); Always On (-40%); Emanation (-20%); Accessibility, limited to warp-sensitive targets only (-20%, the same kind of narrowing this project's Grey Knights Banishment entry uses for a daemon-only target restriction). Net: +30+150-40-20-20 = +100%. 10 x 2.0 = 20 points. Carried by the Ravener and Lictor as this project's own house choice (framework.md lists Shadow in the Warp as a Tyranid trait without saying which bioforms carry it); the Codex ties it to synapse creatures and the Hive Mind's psychic presence, which is why the Warrior, Zoanthrope and Hive Tyrant carry it and the mindless Carnifex does not.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mhBSHpdGiws8lSfPZ",
							"name": "Disadvantage Delivered (Warp-Deafened: -3 to psychic skill and Warp Empowerment-related rolls, plus an immediate Fright Check on first exposure, -30 pts)",
							"cost_adj": "30%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mwrS0ytF64UGfuiU0",
							"name": "Area Effect, 8 yards",
							"cost_adj": "+150%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mYs3moxJoR2_VjxMm",
							"name": "Always On",
							"cost_adj": "-40%"
						},
						{
							"id": "myMtVEE3EvnHYILpO",
							"name": "Emanation",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mn_-XJgESR3SSzh9c",
							"name": "Accessibility (only affects psykers and other warp-sensitive minds)",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tehpHCyn5x8D7nlTm",
					"name": "Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction)",
					"reference": "P53",
					"local_notes": "Divisor 2 (level = divisor): after DR and wounding multipliers, all injury is divided by 2 (round down, minimum 1 if anything penetrated).\n\nLore: The Ravener's segmented, muscled body keeps moving through damage. Large synapse-beasts and monsters shrug off wounds that would fell smaller creatures.\n\nDesign: Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction) (GURPS Powers p. 53, 25 points per level, level = divisor): divisor 2 = 50 points. Gives the Ravener the bulk-and-redundant-organs toughness the lore describes on top of its chitin DR and extra HP.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 25,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 2,
					"base_points": 0
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TOF_2fLUflLqAnubZ",
			"name": "Body Lens: Tyranid Warrior",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Tyranid Warrior: the officer caste of the swarm, big and canny enough to hold real tactical initiative and to project the Hive Mind's discipline to the lesser bioforms nearby. Consistent Codex background across editions and Black Library's Hive Fleet fiction (chiefly the Deathwatch novels' portrayal of Warriors as the dangerous, thinking edge of an otherwise mindless tide).\n\nDesign: 303 (Attribute Modifiers) + 300 (Chitin DR 60) + 25 (Synapse Creature) + 25 (Shadow in the Warp) + 50 (Injury Tolerance 2) = 703 points, added to Tyranid Biology's 20 for 723 points of physiology alone before skills and its weapon-symbiotes -- in framework.md's Astartes point tier (550-850), matching a Warrior's role as the swarm's single most dangerous common bioform. See the Tyranid Warrior template. NPC creature stat block only; do not read this total as a measure of individual threat the way a similarly-costed human character would be -- a lone Warrior separated from its swarm and any synapse backup is a smaller problem than the number suggests.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Tyranids"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tRRn1bhmX0ABMzsJN",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Tyranid Warrior)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target: SM +1, ST 34, DX 13, IQ 8, HT 14, HP 40. Increased ST +24 (B14, 10/level, 240) for ST 34 (strength ladder: level with an Ork Nob), with the SM +1 discount: 240 x 0.9 = 216. Increased DX +3 (B15, 20/level, 60) for DX 13. Decreased IQ -2 (B16, 20/level, -40) for IQ 8 -- a genuine officer of the swarm, sharp enough for real tactical judgement without approaching a human genius. Increased HT +4 (40) for HT 14. Extra Hit Points +6 (B16, 2/level, 12) on top of the ST-34 base of 34, for HP 40. Increased Basic Move +3 (B17, 5/level, 15): Basic Speed 6.75 gives Move 6, so +3 lands Basic Move at 9. 216+60-40+40+12+15 = 303.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 303,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "sm",
							"amount": 1
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": 24
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "dx",
							"amount": 3
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": -2
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "ht",
							"amount": 4
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "hp",
							"amount": 6
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "basic_move",
							"amount": 3
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tOvhYCLBnPTNXpudI",
					"name": "Chitin (Tyranid Warrior)",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 60, all locations. Weak Points 5 (as a Ravener's, matching framework.md's own figure for this tier): on a hit that fails to penetrate, roll 3d; 5 or less halves DR (30). Eye clusters run at DR 30 rather than 60.\n\nLore: A Tyranid's exoskeleton is grown, not worn: dense bio-ceramic chitin fused directly to the flesh beneath it, thickest on the largest bioforms and thin enough on a Termagant to be more like a swarm-trooper's flak jacket than real armour plate. Universal across Codex background and every piece of Black Library Tyranid fiction.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance (B46, 5/level, all locations): natural DR bought directly on the body, per framework.md's explicit instruction that chitin is a character trait, not equipment. Each body-type lens overrides `levels`/`dr` to its own figure from framework.md's Tyranid anchor table; see that lens's own copy of this trait for its point cost.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 60,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"all"
							],
							"amount": 60
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "t0ZCAudsr6kOSX1-M",
					"name": "Synapse Creature (Warrior)",
					"reference": "B35",
					"local_notes": "Every friendly Tyranid organism within 16 yards automatically gains Synapse Discipline's benefit and has Synapse Dependence suppressed, for as long as this creature is alive, conscious, and itself linked (directly or through a closer synapse creature) to the Hive Mind. A Tyranid Warrior's own Synapse Dependence is never active while it is drawing on the wider Hive Mind this way.\n\nLore: Tyranid Warriors are the swarm's front-line officers precisely because they project this field: Codex background across editions names Warriors, alongside larger synapse-capable bioforms, as the link that keeps a local swarm fighting with purpose instead of degenerating into a feeding frenzy the instant a fight turns bad.\n\nDesign: Affliction (B35) base 10; Advantage Delivered (a 10-point granted advantage, valued at +10% per this project's own Advantage/ Disadvantage Delivered convention); Area Effect at the 16-yard tier (+200%, B102); Always On (-40%, B113, it cannot be switched off); Emanation (-20%, B111, proximity only, no attack roll). Net: +10+200-40-20 = +150%. 10 x 2.5 = 25 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mT1fXgeC9yMFnSZkv",
							"name": "Advantage Delivered (grants Synapse Discipline, 10 pts)",
							"cost_adj": "10%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mExV3pzFJ7WCEv1V0",
							"name": "Area Effect, 16 yards",
							"cost_adj": "+200%"
						},
						{
							"id": "moMxCtheXmRNmX6uo",
							"name": "Always On",
							"cost_adj": "-40%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mjYb6MDnOEZOaYoLa",
							"name": "Emanation",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tGdBZPjmn34nPhZvG",
					"name": "Shadow in the Warp (Tyranid Warrior)",
					"reference": "B35",
					"local_notes": "Any psyker within 16 yards suffers -3 to Psyniscience, Warp Sorcery-tagged skill rolls, and the Will roll to resist Perils of the Warp, and must make an immediate Fright Check the first time they come within range.\n\nLore: A Warrior's synapse role means it carries a heavier share of the Hive Mind's psychic mass than a lesser predator bioform, widening its Shadow in the Warp to match its synapse range.\n\nDesign: Affliction (B35) base 10; Disadvantage Delivered (+30%); Area Effect at the 16-yard tier (+200%, B102); Always On (-40%); Emanation (-20%); Accessibility (-20%). Net: +30+200-40-20-20 = +150%. 10 x 2.5 = 25 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mbeH40W3OTnImfvCo",
							"name": "Disadvantage Delivered (Warp-Deafened: -3 to psychic skill and Warp Empowerment-related rolls, plus an immediate Fright Check on first exposure, -30 pts)",
							"cost_adj": "30%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mshLGYq4SSygNtOtE",
							"name": "Area Effect, 16 yards",
							"cost_adj": "+200%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mWBzHQHB9dvU8zACi",
							"name": "Always On",
							"cost_adj": "-40%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mC7bqv1RWTpdPOkR6",
							"name": "Emanation",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mItguhPS7HddWPHqY",
							"name": "Accessibility (only affects psykers and other warp-sensitive minds)",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tH3z2uHfQOK72GSnW",
					"name": "Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction)",
					"reference": "P53",
					"local_notes": "Divisor 2 (level = divisor): after DR and wounding multipliers, all injury is divided by 2 (round down, minimum 1 if anything penetrated).\n\nLore: A Warrior's redundant organs and dense muscle let it fight on through wounds. Large synapse-beasts and monsters shrug off wounds that would fell smaller creatures.\n\nDesign: Injury Tolerance (Damage Reduction) (GURPS Powers p. 53, 25 points per level, level = divisor): divisor 2 = 50 points. Gives the Warrior the bulk-and-redundant-organs toughness the lore describes on top of its chitin DR and extra HP.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Tyranids"
					],
					"points_per_level": 25,
